<%# The row actions, defined once and rendered twice: in the trailing cell at lg, and inside the
    primary cell below it, where the table is one column. Two definitions of one row's actions is how
    the two widths would come to offer different things.

    Duplicated in the DOM rather than moved by CSS, which is what the trading floor does with Buy/Sell.
    Safe here because these are links: the copy that is not in play is `display: none`, so it is out of
    the accessibility tree and Capybara will not match it either. It would **not** be safe for a form
    control - two inputs with the same name submit twice - which is why the grade book reflows instead.

    A vague "Are you sure?" gives no basis for the decision, so the confirmation names the record.

    **Delete only, and the same holds on the six indexes that write their own actions.** `View` went when
    the name in the primary cell became a link to the record's page; `Edit` went for the same reason once
    that page started editing in place - a row was offering two controls to one destination. What stays on
    a row is what a link cannot do: Delete, and the archive/restore pair the archivable indexes add. %>
<%= ghost_action_link "Delete", [:admin, route_model(record)],
                      icon: "trash-2", variant: :danger,
                      data: { turbo_method: :delete, turbo_confirm: delete_confirm(record) } %>
